# {{.Name}}

A [Warren](https://github.com/MerseniBilel/warren) service.

## Run it
{{- if eq .DB "postgres" }}

Migrate first. It is a DEPLOY STEP, run once before the new replicas start,
never a lifecycle hook — `cmd/migrate/main.go` says why in full.

```bash
export {{.EnvPrefix}}_DATABASE_URL='postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/{{.Name}}?sslmode=disable'
go run ./cmd/migrate
```

Then:

```bash
{{- if eq .Broker "kafka" }}
export {{.EnvPrefix}}_KAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:9092
{{- end }}
go run ./cmd/{{.Name}}
```

It listens on 8080; `{{.EnvPrefix}}_HTTP_PORT` moves it, which is what lets a
second replica run on the same host.

It boots the module graph, opens the pool, starts the outbox relay and the
notification consumer, opens readiness, and drains cleanly on SIGTERM.
Without `{{.EnvPrefix}}_DATABASE_URL`{{if eq .Broker "kafka"}} or
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_KAFKA_BROKERS`{{end}} it fails at boot naming the variable — every
error the framework can detect surfaces at boot, never on request 1.
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_NAME` needs no value here: it defaults to `{{.Name}}`, so a
fresh checkout runs. Set it per environment.
{{- else }}

```bash
go run ./cmd/{{.Name}}
```

It boots the module graph, starts the outbox relay and the notification
consumer, opens readiness, and drains cleanly on SIGTERM. It needs no
environment at all to start: `{{.EnvPrefix}}_NAME` defaults to `{{.Name}}`.
Every error the framework *can* detect still surfaces at boot, never on
request 1.
{{- end }}

## Test it

```bash
go test -race ./...
```

{{- if eq .DB "postgres" }}
`internal/modules/user/module_test.go` boots the whole graph and drives the
handler through `App.Invoke`, then asserts the event reached the broker
through the outbox relay. It needs a database, so it SKIPS unless
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_DATABASE_URL` is set — and it truncates the tables it uses
first, because a test that shares a real database with the last run is a
test that passes once.
{{- else }}
`internal/modules/user/module_test.go` boots the whole graph and drives the
handler through `App.Invoke`, then asserts the event reached the broker
through the outbox relay. No Docker, no network, no sleeps.
{{- end }}

## What it serves

```
POST /users     the RegisterUser use case             201
GET  /healthz   liveness — runs no checks             200
GET  /readyz    readiness — lifecycle + every check   200 / 503
```

Every response carries `X-Correlation-Id`; a validation failure is a 400 with
per-field `details`. None of that is code in this repository — it comes from
the controller registering one route.

## What is not here yet
{{- if eq .DB "postgres" }}

**No gRPC adapter.** Postgres IS wired: `internal/platform/module.go`
declares `Postgres()` with the outbox and inbox stores, the user repository
is plain SQL, and `cmd/migrate` applies both schemas.

A feature you add with `warren g` imports **two** platform modules —
`platform.Module()` for the broker and the unit of work, and
`platform.Postgres()` for `postgres.DB` and `inbox.Store`. `warren g
repository --driver postgres` and `warren g consumer` add the second one for
you. Exports name what a module PROVIDES, which is why `platform.Module()`
cannot re-export the adapter's ports on your behalf.
{{- else }}

**No gRPC adapter**, and no Postgres wiring: the repository is in-memory.
Scaffold with `warren new <name> --db postgres` to get it wired from the
start — that writes the adapter module, a SQL repository, `cmd/migrate` and
the first migration. Adding it by hand afterwards means removing the memory
unit of work and the memory outbox and inbox stores as well, or the boot
fails with an ambiguous binding.

Everything else works: use cases, the module graph, config, the transactional
outbox, the consumer chain, health.
{{- end }}

## Layout

```
cmd/{{.Name}}/            main: the module graph and Run()
internal/config/          the app's Config struct
internal/platform/        broker, outbox, unit of work, relay, health
internal/modules/user/    a feature: domain, application, infrastructure
internal/modules/notification/
                          a second feature, consuming the first's events
```

`internal/modules/<name>/module.go` is the only file permitted to see all
four layers. `domain` imports nothing from the others — that rule is why the
domain can be tested, versioned, and extracted on its own.
